# Labour CESS presenter deck standards

Audience: Government of Karnataka / Board committee — mixed, often non-technical. Every scene, beat, and speaker note must follow this.

## Language

- Use **formal government English** in **short, complete sentences**.
- **Avoid big / long sentences.** Prefer one idea per sentence. If a line wraps twice on the slide, split it.
- Aim for roughly **8–14 words** per on-screen sentence where possible; supporting lines stay to **one short line**.
- Write for a **common man** — no developer or IT jargon on slides.
- Prefer plain words over technical ones:

| Avoid on slides | Prefer |
|-----------------|--------|
| API, REST, SOAP, SFTP, Queue | Direct link, department exchange, file transfer, message pass |
| Connector, middleware protocol | Periodic transfer / feed |
| Manual entry | Officer entry / authorised entry |
| Map & transform, route & retry | Bring details into one format; send again if a transfer fails |
| Audit trail, source reference | Keep a full exchange record; keep where each detail came from |
| Board system, CESS portal (as the app) | See lexicon below |
| Fraud / confirmed leakage | Potential exception (unless Board has decided) |

## Application lexicon (one name only)

| Term | Means |
|------|--------|
| **Labour CESS Tracking & Monitoring System** | Full official name of this application |
| **Central Platform** | Short form of the same application only |
| **Smart Middleware** | Linking layer (M06) — not a separate product name for the whole app |
| **Labour CESS Portal** | Builder / agency collection channel — not the main app |
| **KSK** | Separate Board service platform |
| **The Board / KBOCWWB** | The institution — not software |
| **Worker welfare** | Purpose of CESS funds — not a system name |

Never say “Board system”, “CESS portal”, or “Worker Welfare system” when you mean the Central Platform.

## Visualization over text

- Design **visualization-first**: icons, flows, hubs, stamps, short chips.
- One idea per section; one short supporting sentence under the title.
- Avoid paragraphs, long lists, and dense console prose on the main slide.
- Put long RFP lists behind a badge / dialog, not on the face of the slide.
- Prefer icon + 2–5 word label over a sentence when the meaning is clear.

### No cards by default (Scene 01 lesson)

- **Do not** fill the slide with bordered list cards, white row boxes, or “dashboard panels” for every item.
- Prefer **icon stamps** (circle + short label), flow lines / packets, and one shaped hub plaque.
- Cards are allowed only when they hold a real interaction (dialog, clickable rail) or a single destination plaque — not as a grid of repeating boxes.
- When densifying empty space, **tighten the picture** — do not regress into card grids.

### Story as a picture

- Layout must **read the story**: e.g. many offices → construction project → gap / Smart Middleware → one Board file.
- Reference tone: `1.png` / `2.png` in the repo (sources flow into ABC site, then into the platform) — not a spreadsheet of widgets.
- Prefer **one continuous composition** (left → centre → right, or constellation → hub). Do not bolt a “problem UI” and a “solution UI” as two unrelated screens.
- When merging scenes, **keep order**: problem beats first, then solution beats; do not drop ideas.

### Pleasant visuals

- Prefer a **light stage** (white / mist) with soft rings and calm shadows for narrative scenes — avoid a heavy full-bleed navy box unless the story needs drama.
- Hub / step plaques: white or soft surface + **colour accent** (icon wrap, ring, dots) so each journey step is colour-coded without loud solid slabs.
- Site photo: keep the project visible; use a light gradient veil for captions — do not bury the picture under dark overlays.
- **Watch for / Key message / Next** live in a **footer band under the stage**, not stacked on top of the photo. Soft chips on mist / soft tints — not harsh full-width alert bars.

## Animation (more animated, purposeful)

- Prefer an **animated presentation**: motion should explain the story (flow in, connect, receive, highlight) — not decorate randomly.
- Each scene should ship **at least 2–3 intentional motions** (e.g. Reveal on beat, Stagger on stamps, flow packets / arrows, gentle hub pulse).
- Use existing **Motion + SlideKit** patterns: `Reveal`, `Stagger`, `IconChip`; add scene-local `motion` for flows and emphasis.
- Advance story with **beats (Space)** — animate what appears; do not dump the full layout at once.
- Respect **reduced motion** (`useReducedMotion`) — still readable when animation is off.
- Avoid noisy loops, excessive bounce, glow spam, or animation that hides text.

## Scene craft

- Absolute scene title; supporting line in plain GoK language.
- Progressive beats (Space) — reveal the visual story, then key message, then Next.
- Key message as a short sentence + icon chain, not a speech.
- Colour-code multi-step journeys (Middleware · Receiving · Match · Check · One file) so Space advances feel distinct.
- Keep Motion + Phosphor patterns already in `SlideKit` / scenes.

## Visual QA (do this when polishing visuals)

- Capture the live slide (headless Chrome or user screenshot) and **read the image** before claiming the design is done.
- Check: story readable in one glance; no card spam; footer not colliding with the stage; hub not covering sources.

